[agent:result] === synced-tables.md ===
[agent:result] # Lakebase synced tables
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Official docs:** https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/sync-tables
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Lakebase synced tables sync data from Unity Catalog Delta tables into Lakebase as PostgreSQL tables for OLTP access patterns. Previously known as **Reverse ETL**.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **How it works:** Synced tables create a managed copy — a Unity Catalog table (read-only, managed by sync pipeline) and a Postgres table in Lakebase (queryable by apps). Uses managed Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Performance (per Autoscaling CU):**
[agent:result] - Continuous/Triggered: ~150 rows/sec per CU
[agent:result] - Snapshot: ~2,000 rows/sec per CU
[agent:result] - Each synced table uses up to 16 connections
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Sync Modes
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] | Mode | Description | CDF Required | Best For |
[agent:result] |------|-------------|-------------|----------|
[agent:result] | **Snapshot** | One-time full copy | No | Initial setup, small tables, >10% data change |
[agent:result] | **Triggered** | Scheduled updates | Yes | Dashboards updated hourly/daily |
[agent:result] | **Continuous** | Real-time (seconds latency, 15s min interval) | Yes | Live applications |
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Enable CDF on source table:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```sql
[agent:result] ALTER TABLE your_catalog.your_schema.your_table
[agent:result] SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true)
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Prerequisites
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] - A Databricks workspace with Lakebase enabled
[agent:result] - An active Lakebase project with a branch and endpoint
[agent:result] - A Unity Catalog source table to sync
[agent:result] - **Permissions:** `USE_SCHEMA` and `CREATE_TABLE` on the target schema
[agent:result] - For Triggered/Continuous modes: Change Data Feed enabled on the source table
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Note:** Your Lakebase database must be registered as a UC catalog (one-time setup per project). Skip if already done.
[agent:result] >
[agent:result] > ```bash
[agent:result] > databricks postgres create-catalog <CATALOG_NAME> \
[agent:result] >   --json '{
[agent:result] >     "spec": {
[agent:result] >       "postgres_database": "<POSTGRES_DATABASE>",
[agent:result] >       "branch": "projects/<PROJECT_ID>/branches/<BRANCH_ID>"
[agent:result] >     }
[agent:result] >   }' --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] > ```
[agent:result] >
[agent:result] > The `<POSTGRES_DATABASE>` is the Postgres database name (default: `databricks_postgres`), not the resource path.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Creating Lakebase synced tables
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Source table must exist first.** Synced tables sync from an existing UC table, view, or materialized view. If the source needs transformation, ask the user how they want to prepare it (DLT materialized view, regular view, or existing table). Do not run ad-hoc `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT` statements.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres create-synced-table <LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.<SCHEMA>.<TABLE> \
[agent:result]   --json '{
[agent:result]     "spec": {
[agent:result]       "source_table_full_name": "analytics.gold.user_profiles",
[agent:result]       "primary_key_columns": ["user_id"],
[agent:result]       "scheduling_policy": "TRIGGERED",
[agent:result]       "branch": "projects/<PROJECT_ID>/branches/production",
[agent:result]       "postgres_database": "databricks_postgres",
[agent:result]       "create_database_objects_if_missing": true,
[agent:result]       "new_pipeline_spec": {
[agent:result]         "storage_catalog": "<REGULAR_UC_CATALOG>",
[agent:result]         "storage_schema": "default"
[agent:result]       }
[agent:result]     }
[agent:result]   }' --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] | Field | Required | Description |
[agent:result] |-------|----------|-------------|
[agent:result] | `source_table_full_name` | Yes | Full Unity Catalog name of the source table |
[agent:result] | `primary_key_columns` | Yes | Column(s) forming the primary key |
[agent:result] | `scheduling_policy` | Yes | `SNAPSHOT`, `TRIGGERED`, or `CONTINUOUS` |
[agent:result] | `branch` | Yes | Target Lakebase branch (`projects/<PROJECT_ID>/branches/<BRANCH_ID>`) |
[agent:result] | `postgres_database` | Yes | Postgres database name (default: `databricks_postgres`), not the resource path |
[agent:result] | `create_database_objects_if_missing` | No | Auto-create Postgres schema/database if missing (default: `false`) |
[agent:result] | `new_pipeline_spec.storage_catalog` | Yes | A **regular** UC catalog for DLT pipeline metadata (NOT the Lakebase catalog) |
[agent:result] | `new_pipeline_spec.storage_schema` | Yes | Schema in the storage catalog for pipeline metadata (e.g. `default`) |
[agent:result] | `timeseries_key` | No | Column for deduplication when source has duplicate PKs (latest wins). Performance penalty. |
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Note:** Nulls in PK columns are excluded from sync.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Long-running operation; CLI waits by default. Use `--no-wait` to return immediately.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Supported source types:** managed/external Delta tables, managed/external Iceberg tables, views, and materialized views.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Check status:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres get-synced-table "synced_tables/<LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.<SCHEMA>.<TABLE>" --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Delete:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres delete-synced-table "synced_tables/<LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.<SCHEMA>.<TABLE>" --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Deletes the sync pipeline and the UC table entry. The Postgres table remains and must be dropped manually if no longer needed (`DROP TABLE <schema>.<table>`).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **DABs:** The bundle schema includes `synced_database_tables`, but it maps to the Provisioned Terraform resource (`databricks_database_synced_database_table`), not the Autoscaling API. **Do not use `synced_database_tables` in DABs with Autoscaling projects** — it routes through the Provisioned API and may create unintended Provisioned instances. DAB support for Autoscaling synced tables (`postgres_synced_tables`) is blocked on Terraform provider work and not yet available. **For Autoscaling projects, use the CLI commands above.**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Example: Sync NYC Taxi Data to Lakebase
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Sync the `samples.nyctaxi.trips` sample table into Lakebase for low-latency app queries.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **1. Register a UC catalog** (if not already done — see Prerequisites above).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **2. Create the synced table (Snapshot mode):**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres create-synced-table <LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.public.nyc_trips \
[agent:result]   --json '{
[agent:result]     "spec": {
[agent:result]       "source_table_full_name": "samples.nyctaxi.trips",
[agent:result]       "primary_key_columns": ["tpep_pickup_datetime", "tpep_dropoff_datetime", "pickup_zip", "dropoff_zip"],
[agent:result]       "scheduling_policy": "SNAPSHOT",
[agent:result]       "branch": "projects/<PROJECT_ID>/branches/production",
[agent:result]       "postgres_database": "databricks_postgres",
[agent:result]       "create_database_objects_if_missing": true,
[agent:result]       "new_pipeline_spec": {
[agent:result]         "storage_catalog": "<REGULAR_UC_CATALOG>",
[agent:result]         "storage_schema": "default"
[agent:result]       }
[agent:result]     }
[agent:result]   }' --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Note:** `samples.nyctaxi.trips` has no single unique column, so a composite primary key is used. Snapshot mode is chosen here — Triggered/Continuous require CDF enabled on the source table.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **3. Check sync status:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres get-synced-table "synced_tables/<LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.public.nyc_trips" --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **4. Query from Postgres once synced:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```sql
[agent:result] SELECT pickup_zip, COUNT(*) AS trip_count, AVG(fare_amount) AS avg_fare
[agent:result] FROM public.nyc_trips
[agent:result] GROUP BY pickup_zip
[agent:result] ORDER BY trip_count DESC
[agent:result] LIMIT 10;
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **5. Clean up:**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] databricks postgres delete-synced-table "synced_tables/<LAKEBASE_CATALOG>.public.nyc_trips" --profile <PROFILE>
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## App Access
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] If a Databricks App reads synced tables, the app's Service Principal needs explicit GRANT access. See the lakebase skill's SKILL.md "Grant app SP access to synced tables" section for the SQL commands and connection steps.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Data Type Mapping
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] | Unity Catalog Type | Postgres Type |
[agent:result] |-------------------|---------------|
[agent:result] | BIGINT | BIGINT |
[agent:result] | BINARY | BYTEA |
[agent:result] | BOOLEAN | BOOLEAN |
[agent:result] | DATE | DATE |
[agent:result] | DECIMAL(p,s) | NUMERIC |
[agent:result] | DOUBLE | DOUBLE PRECISION |
[agent:result] | FLOAT | REAL |
[agent:result] | INT | INTEGER |
[agent:result] | INTERVAL | INTERVAL |
[agent:result] | SMALLINT | SMALLINT |
[agent:result] | STRING | TEXT |
[agent:result] | TIMESTAMP | TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE |
[agent:result] | TIMESTAMP_NTZ | TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE |
[agent:result] | TINYINT | SMALLINT |
[agent:result] | ARRAY, MAP, STRUCT | JSONB |
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Unsupported:** GEOGRAPHY, GEOMETRY, VARIANT, OBJECT
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Capacity Planning
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] - **Connections:** Each synced table uses up to 16 connections toward the endpoint limit
[agent:result] - **Storage:** 8 TB logical data per branch (synced tables count toward the branch storage limit)
[agent:result] - **Recommendation:** Keep individual tables under 1 TB if they require incremental refreshes
[agent:result] - **Connections (instance):** 1,000 max concurrent connections per instance
[agent:result] - **Naming:** Database, schema, and table names allow `[A-Za-z0-9_]+` only
[agent:result] - **Schema evolution:** Only additive changes (adding columns) for Triggered/Continuous modes
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Cost guidance:**
[agent:result] - **Continuous mode:** Reuse pipelines for ~10 tables/pipeline — roughly 10x cheaper per table than separate pipelines
[agent:result] - **Cost formula:** `[Rows / (Speed × CUs × 3600)] × DLT Hourly Rate` (check current DLT pricing for your cloud/region)
[agent:result] - **Snapshot vs incremental:** Snapshot is ~10x faster when >10% of data changes per cycle
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Lakehouse Sync (Beta)
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Reverse direction: continuously streams changes **from** Lakebase Postgres **into** Unity Catalog Delta tables using CDC (SCD Type 2 history). Destination tables are named `lb_<table_name>_history`. Does not require external compute, pipelines, or jobs — it is a native Lakebase feature. Available on AWS, Azure, and GCP.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] > **Important:** Tables must reside in the `databricks_postgres` database for Lakehouse Sync to work.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Lakehouse Sync enablement is a UI-only action** — configured via the "Lakehouse sync" tab in the branch overview, not via CLI or API. It operates at the **schema level**: once enabled, all current and future tables in that schema sync to Unity Catalog. When automating CDC workflows, treat this as a manual post-automation step and inform the user.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Prerequisites:**
[agent:result] - Lakebase Autoscaling project running **Postgres 17**
[agent:result] - Tables must reside in the `databricks_postgres` database
[agent:result] - `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` must be set on all source tables before enabling sync:
[agent:result]   ```sql
[agent:result]   ALTER TABLE <schema>.<table> REPLICA IDENTITY FULL;
[agent:result]   ```
[agent:result] - Verify replica identity:
[agent:result]   ```sql
[agent:result]   SELECT n.nspname AS schema, c.relname AS table_name,
[agent:result]          CASE c.relreplident WHEN 'f' THEN 'full' WHEN 'd' THEN 'default' WHEN 'n' THEN 'nothing' END AS replica_identity
[agent:result]   FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
[agent:result]   WHERE c.relkind = 'r' AND n.nspname = 'public';
[agent:result]   ```
[agent:result] - **Permissions:** CAN MANAGE on source project; USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + CREATE TABLE on destination
[agent:result] - Catalogs with default storage are **unsupported**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Limitations:**
[agent:result] - Partitioned tables are not supported
[agent:result] - Disabling and re-enabling sync does **not** re-snapshot — missing changes are lost permanently
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] For the full Lakehouse Sync reference, see [lakehouse-sync.md](lakehouse-sync.md). For building medallion pipelines from CDC history, see [medallion-from-cdc.md](medallion-from-cdc.md).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Use Cases
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Product catalog:** Sync gold-tier product data to Lakebase for low-latency web app reads. Use Triggered mode for hourly/daily updates.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Real-time feature serving:** Sync ML feature tables to Lakebase with Continuous mode for sub-second feature lookups during inference.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Best Practices
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] 1. **Sync gold/aggregated tables, not raw tables.** Synced tables are for serving pre-curated data at OLTP speed. If your app needs aggregations (GROUP BY, JOINs across large tables), create a gold Delta table or materialized view first, then sync that. Syncing raw tables and aggregating in Postgres defeats the latency benefit — Postgres is not optimized for OLAP workloads on millions of rows.
[agent:result] 2. Enable CDF on source tables before creating Triggered/Continuous syncs
[agent:result] 3. Snapshot mode is ~10x faster than incremental when >10% of data changes per cycle
[agent:result] 4. Monitor sync status for failures and latency via Catalog Explorer
[agent:result] 5. Create indexes in Postgres for your application query patterns
[agent:result] 6. Account for the 16-connection-per-table limit when planning endpoint capacity
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Constraints
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] - **Read-only in Postgres:** Only SELECT queries, CREATE INDEX, and DROP TABLE are allowed on synced tables. Any data modifications (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) corrupt the sync pipeline.
[agent:result] - **Null bytes:** Null bytes (0x00) in STRING, ARRAY, MAP, or STRUCT columns cause sync failures. Sanitize source data: `REPLACE(col, CAST(CHAR(0) AS STRING), '')`.
[agent:result] - **Unsupported types:** GEOGRAPHY, GEOMETRY, VARIANT, OBJECT columns cannot be synced.
[agent:result] - **FGAC not propagated:** Fine-grained access control (row filters, column masks) from Unity Catalog is not propagated to synced tables. **Workaround:** Create a view on the source table with the desired filter (`SELECT * FROM table WHERE ...`), then sync the view in Snapshot mode. Caveat: the sync runs as the creator and only sees their visible rows.
